{
  "name": "Bouncing ball",
  "description": "A ball thrown straight up from the floor at 20 m/s, falling back under gravity and bouncing with 0.9 of the speed it landed with: the classic test of how a solver handles events. Height is the one compartment. Bounce is a trigger that fires when Height falls through zero, so the solver stops exactly there and starts again. Each bounce takes two snapshots, its time (T_bounce) and the speed the ball leaves the floor with (V_up), and between bounces the ball's Velocity is V_up − g·(t − T_bounce).\n\nThe landing speed is read off the flight time: a flight from the floor and back takes 2V/g, so the ball lands at g times half its time in the air. That keeps V_up from reading its own value, which a snapshot may not do. The bounces come where the closed form (2·v0/g)·(1 − eⁿ)/(1 − e) puts them: 4.08, 7.75 and 11.05 s, and so on, twelve in the thirty seconds, with the energy falling to e² = 0.81 of itself at each.\n\nTry: run it to 45 s. The bounces pile up towards (2·v0/g)/(1 − e) = 40.8 s, where infinitely many of them would happen in a finite time (Zeno behaviour). After about a hundred, the flights are too short to resolve and the ball falls through the floor. That is a property of the model, which needs a rule for coming to rest, not a fault in the solver.",
  "simulation": {
    "start_time": 0,
    "end_time": 30,
    "output_points": 601,
    "spacing": "linear",
    "solver": "dp45",
    "rtol": 1e-08,
    "abstol": 1e-10,
    "non_negative": false,
    "time_unit": "second"
  },
  "parameters": [
    {
      "name": "g",
      "value": 9.81,
      "unit": "m/second^2"
    },
    {
      "name": "v0",
      "value": 20,
      "unit": "m/second"
    },
    {
      "name": "restitution",
      "value": 0.9
    }
  ],
  "compartments": [
    {
      "name": "Height",
